{"name":"Allspice Berry","hex":"#8e443d","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"allspice","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A spice; the dried and ground unripe fruit of Pimenta dioica, thought to combine the flavours of several spices, such as cinnamon, nutmeg and cloves.","Pimenta dioica, an evergreen tree of tropical America with aromatic berries."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/allspice"},{"term":"berry","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A small succulent fruit, of any one of many varieties.","A soft fruit which develops from a single ovary and contains seeds not encased in pits."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To pick berries.","To bear or produce berries."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/berry"}],"entries":[{"title":"Allspice","blocks":[{"text":"Allspice, also known as Jamaica pepper, myrtle pepper, pimenta, or pimento, is the dried unripe berry of Pimenta dioica, a midcanopy tree native to the Greater Antilles, southern Mexico, and Central America, now cultivated in many warm parts of the world."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a5/Pimenta_dioica_-_K%C3%B6hler%E2%80%93s_Medizinal-Pflanzen-239.jpg/330px-Pimenta_dioica_-_K%C3%B6hler%E2%80%93s_Medizinal-Pflanzen-239.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Allspice","wikidata":"Q1963621"},{"title":"Berry","blocks":[{"text":"A berry is a small, pulpy, and often edible fruit. Typically, berries are juicy, rounded, brightly colored, sweet, sour or tart, and do not have a stone or pit although many pips or seeds may be present.‍ Common examples of berries in the culinary sense are strawberries, raspberries, blueberries, blackberries, white currants, blackcurrants, and redcurrants.‍ In Britain, soft fruit is a horticultural term for such fruits.‍"}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e4/Berberis_thunbergii_berries.jpg/330px-Berberis_thunbergii_berries.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Berry","wikidata":"Q16128920"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7b/Piment_Flower.jpg/500px-Piment_Flower.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7b/Piment_Flower.jpg/1280px-Piment_Flower.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7b/Piment_Flower.jpg/500px-Piment_Flower.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Piment flower in Uaxactún, north of Tikal National Park, Guatemala","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APiment_Flower.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/39/Pimenta_dioica_%28Allspice%29_Trees.jpg/500px-Pimenta_dioica_%28Allspice%29_Trees.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/39/Pimenta_dioica_%28Allspice%29_Trees.jpg/1280px-Pimenta_dioica_%28Allspice%29_Trees.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/39/Pimenta_dioica_%28Allspice%29_Trees.jpg/500px-Pimenta_dioica_%28Allspice%29_Trees.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"P. dioica mature trees in Guatemala","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APimenta_dioica_(Allspice)_Trees.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d1/AllspiceBowl.JPG/500px-AllspiceBowl.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d1/AllspiceBowl.JPG/1280px-AllspiceBowl.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_conte